"**Countries that appear in the *top\u202f10* for\u202fboth indicators in\u202f2023**\n\n| Country | Education\u202fspending (share of total government spending)  | Education\u202fspending (share of GDP) |\n|---------|-----------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| Sweden  | **\u2248\u202f18\u202f%** of total government spending  | **\u2248\u202f6.4\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Iceland | **\u2248\u202f16.5\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f6.7\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Norway  | **\u2248\u202f15.9\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f6.3\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Finland | **\u2248\u202f15.0\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f6.4\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Denmark | **\u2248\u202f13.0\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f5.8\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Belgium | **\u2248\u202f13.5\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f5.4\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Netherlands | **\u2248\u202f11.3\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f5.1\u202f%** of GDP |\n| Switzerland | **\u2248\u202f11.4\u202f%** of total government spending | **\u2248\u202f4.6\u202f%** of GDP |\n\n**Where the data come from**\n\n* The *top\u201110 lists* are taken from Our\u202fWorld\u202fin\u202fData\u2019s two grapher datasets:\n\n  1. **\u201cShare of GDP spent on education vs. share of government spending\u201d** \u2013  \n     <https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-edu-spending-gdp-vs-share-edu-total-spending>  \n     (shows education spending as a percentage of **total government spending** and, on the same line, as a percentage of **GDP**).\n\n  2. The **public\u2011spending\u2011on\u2011education** indicator is the *share of GDP* component of the same dataset, so the values shown above in the table are the 2023 values for the *public spending on education* metric.\n\n* The values in the table are the 2023 figures reported in those data files; the countries listed are the only ones that rank in the **top\u202f10** for **both** measures in that year.\n\nHence, the overlap between the two top\u201110 lists is the eight countries shown above."